INT. AFTER-HOURS WAX MUSEUM - NIGHT

Wax figures stand in half-light: a bishop, a boxer, a singer mid-note. A janitor's cart is parked by the velvet rope.

JACINTA ROURKE (40s, coveralls, ID badge clipped upside down) mops slowly around the boxer's feet. Her phone sits on the cart, speaker on.

NILS (V.O.)
(over phone, tinny)
You're in?

JACINTA
Been in an hour. They never lock the sprinkler hatch.

NILS (V.O.)
And the display case?

She leans the mop against the boxer. Walks to the singer's display, a glass cube with a gold microphone inside. She taps the glass with a knuckle, listening.

JACINTA
Alarm's cheap. Same model as my garage.

NILS (V.O.)
Don't touch it yet. I want to hear your breathing first.

JACINTA
You're not my doctor.

NILS (V.O.)
I'm your ride, and I'm eating a sandwich in a freezer truck. Humor me.

She exhales. He listens. She takes the microphone cable out of her pocket and holds it up to the light. He swallows and sets down the sandwich.

NILS (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Okay. Go.
